## Purpose

Continue the Customer workstream without bypassing the Shared Identity/runtime gate by implementing non-HTTP mutation services for Visits, Frequent Items and Customer-Item Quote history.

## Included

- Shared Customer-related mutation request contracts.
- Exact scaled4 parsing in the shared fixed-point helper.
- Customer Visit create/update/delete service:
  - date/content validation;
  - optional Contact link with visit-time person snapshot;
  - revision-based optimistic concurrency;
  - historical inactive Contact/employee may remain when unchanged;
  - hard delete writes a structured shared-Audit event in the same D1 batch transaction.
- Customer Frequent Item create/update/delete service:
  - explicit formal Item vs free-text identity;
  - no name-based auto-linking;
  - active formal Item validation for new/relinked entries;
  - exact `updatedAt` concurrency token for the child row.
- Customer-Item Quote mutation service:
  - new commercial quote always creates a new history record;
  - item number/name/spec snapshots are captured;
  - bounded exact quantity/unit-price breaks;
  - existing-record correction is a separate `correctQuote` path using `expectedRevision`;
  - correction stores compact structured before/after Audit in the same D1 batch transaction;
  - Quote hard-delete is deliberately not introduced.
- Audit Core now supports prepared conditional insert statements for atomic domain mutation + Audit batches.
- Zero-dependency source/schema validation added.
- Architecture/TODO documentation updated.
- Version advances to `0.1.22`.

## Audit/cost boundary

Audit remains cost-conscious per BD-053/054. Visit delete stores bounded historical evidence, and Quote correction input is bounded so structured before/after payloads remain within the shared Audit limits rather than turning Audit into a shadow database.

## Runtime boundary

No protected Customer HTTP routes are exposed. No Shared Identity shortcut is created. No production D1/Worker/DNS/R2/GCS resource is modified. CYAccountingWeb and CYInvoice runtime remain untouched.

## Validation state

Source/schema validators are staged. Full TypeScript/npm build and actual D1 mutation/batch acceptance still require the normal networked local/dev runtime gate.

## Next

Continue with the related-record edit/create UI interaction surface using the already-shared Dialog/Drawer/EditableList/EntityPicker foundations. Protected HTTP routing remains gated by Shared Identity browser-session support and local/dev D1/Worker acceptance.

Google's OAuth verification for the Calendar API's sensitive scopes needs a public privacy policy on the verified domain, linked from the home page (#81). This adds one in Japanese at `/privacy/` and in English at `/en/privacy/`, the address the consent screen will use. It is built as a Starlight page, so it has the same look, language switch and search as the documentation.

The policy says:
- that ASIST has no server of its own and what stays on the computer, per system;
- what goes to which outside service, as the documentation lists it;
- in a section on Google user data:
  - the two scopes and what each is used for;
  - that events are read by the app on the user's computer and that tokens are encrypted there;
  - that the relevant events go to the chosen conversation model or Live API when the user asks about the schedule, and that conversations reach the codex or claude CLI in the daily memory curation;
  - what ASIST does not do: sell the data, use it for advertising, train AI/ML models, or let people read it;
  - Google's Limited Use statement and how to revoke access.
- It also covers the site's Google Analytics, children, the source code and changes to the policy.

The landing page links the policy from its footer in every language: Japanese to the Japanese page, the others to the English one, as the documentation does. The documentation's privacy page links it as well.

