## Critical bug
OpenCode plugins were not a first-class Extension kind. When the model was asked to update Superpowers, the only available path was to edit repository `opencode.json`, then potentially commit/push/deploy it.

Runtime Extension setup must not mutate project/source repositories.

## Fix
- adds first-class `plugin` Extensions;
- moves bundled Superpowers out of repository `opencode.json`;
- performs a one-time migration into bot-owned `app-state.json`;
- pins Superpowers to `v6.4.2`;
- materializes approved plugins into the bot-owned OpenCode overlay:
  `<OPENCODE_TELEGRAM_HOME>/.config/opencode-telegram/managed-opencode.json`;
- plugin updates use the existing Telegram approval lifecycle and stable Extension ID;
- config reload uses OpenCode `global.dispose()` rather than killing the process, avoiding auto-restart races;
- plugin update/remove is transactional and rolls app state back if OpenCode cannot reload;
- rejects project/local plugin paths and floating/unpinned versions;
- npm plugins require an exact semver;
- git-backed plugins require a semantic-version tag or full commit SHA;
- explicitly teaches model-facing tool guidance and AGENTS policy that Extension changes are runtime configuration, not Git/source changes.

## Regression coverage
- Superpowers migrates exactly once to bot-owned state at v6.4.2;
- removal remains removed after restart (migration marker prevents silent reseed);
- plugins are written only to the bot-managed OpenCode overlay;
- repository `opencode.json` contains no plugin entry;
- local/floating plugin sources are rejected;
- changing Superpowers v6.3.0 → v6.4.2 requires approval and updates bot state through the runtime reload path;
- startup test contracts updated for the migration/store dependencies.

